{
  "module": "The transformer block",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "preln-block",
    "title": "The pre-LN transformer block",
    "summary": "How one block is assembled from two sublayers, each in a residual branch with a LayerNorm in front, and what each sublayer is for: attention mixes across positions, the feed-forward step works within one position.",
    "covers": ["preln-block", "attention-vs-ffn-roles"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "residual-stream",
        "reason": "Each sublayer sits in a residual branch."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "layernorm",
        "reason": "Each sublayer's input is normalised first."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "feed-forward",
        "reason": "One of the two sublayers."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "multi-head",
        "reason": "The other sublayer."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "two-sublayers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Now the parts can be put together. One transformer "
            },
            {
              "text": "block",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " has two "
            },
            {
              "text": "sublayers",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": multi-head "
            },
            {
              "text": "attention",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", and the "
            },
            {
              "text": "feed-forward",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " step you have just seen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Each is wrapped in a residual connection, and each is preceded by a "
            },
            {
              "text": "LayerNorm",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 60)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "equations",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The block, for input x, in two steps:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "h = x + \\mathrm{MHA}(\\mathrm{LN}(x))",
          "spoken": "h equals x plus multi-head attention of LayerNorm of x"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "y = h + \\mathrm{FFN}(\\mathrm{LN}(h))",
          "spoken": "y equals h plus FFN of LayerNorm of h"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "First the attention sublayer adds its output to x, then the feed-forward sublayer adds its output to the result."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 60)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "the-picture",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The same block as a picture."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/schematics/transformer-block.svg",
          "alt": "Pre-LayerNorm block. The input x goes through LayerNorm and attention, and the result is added to x to give h. Then h goes through LayerNorm and the feed-forward network, and the result is added to h to give y. Input and output are both n by d_model.",
          "display": "expandable"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The picture says the attention is causal self-attention, the masked kind from earlier. Its label 'post-LN' refers to the original paper's layout, which the last lesson of this module compares with this one."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 60)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "norm-placement",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Look at where the LayerNorm sits. It is applied to the "
            },
            {
              "text": "sublayer's input",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", inside the branch. It is "
            },
            {
              "text": "not",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " applied to the residual sum."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "So the path along the top of the picture, the residual stream, is never touched by a norm. It only ever has sublayer outputs added to it. This layout is called "
            },
            {
              "text": "pre-LN",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 60, 62)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "attention-role",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What is each sublayer for? "
            },
            {
              "text": "Attention mixes information across positions.",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " It is the only place in the whole block where different tokens exchange information."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 59)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "ffn-role",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "feed-forward sublayer works across the feature dimensions within a single position",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". It applies a nonlinear transformation to each token independently."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "So a block does two different things in turn: it moves information between tokens, and then it transforms what each token holds."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 59)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
